# PART I — PRODUCT VISION AND CORE EXPERIENCE

## 2. THE PROBLEM THIS APPLICATION MUST SOLVE

Files on a computer are often scattered across many locations:

- Downloads.
- Pictures.
- Camera folders.
- Screenshots.
- Desktop.
- Documents.
- Videos.
- Music.
- WhatsApp media folders, when present.
- External storage.
- USB drives.
- Custom folders.
- Other application-created directories.

Finding a particular photograph, video, document, or audio file can require opening several folders and manually inspecting their contents.

I want to eliminate that inconvenience.

The application must provide two complementary ways to interact with files.

### Experience A: Browse by category

I should be able to click Photos and immediately browse the images discovered in all configured locations.

I should also be able to narrow the collection to Screenshots, Camera, Downloads, Recently Added, Favourites, and other meaningful categories.

The same principle applies to videos, music, audio recordings, documents, archives, and other supported file types.

### Experience B: Browse the actual filesystem

I must still be able to navigate real folders, drives, directories, and file paths.

I should be able to create folders, rename files, copy and move items, inspect properties, open files with Windows applications, and reveal their original locations.

### The relationship between these experiences

The category-based libraries must be virtual views of existing files, not separate copies of those files.

For example, a photograph stored in:

`C:\Users\User\Pictures\Screenshots\example.png`

should be discoverable through All Photos, Screenshots, Recent Files, search results, and Favourites if I star it.

These views should reference the same underlying file.

Do not duplicate or relocate the original photograph merely to make it appear in multiple categories.

The application should make this relationship clear and preserve the original filesystem structure unless I explicitly request a change.

