## 5. TYPOGRAPHY SYSTEM

Typography must be treated as a core design element.

Use a professional, highly readable typeface that works well on Windows. Consider Segoe UI Variable or another suitable system-compatible family.

Define a consistent typography scale.

Suggested starting values:

| Element | Suggested size |
|---|---:|
| Main page heading | 26–30 px |
| Section heading | 18–21 px |
| Dialog heading | 18–22 px |
| File and folder names | 13–15 px |
| Primary interface text | 13–14 px |
| Secondary metadata | 11–12 px |
| Toolbar labels | 12–13 px |
| Small status information | 11–12 px |
| Keyboard shortcuts | 11–12 px |

These values are starting points, not rigid requirements. Adjust them to the chosen framework, display scaling, and Windows conventions.

Use a consistent line-height system.

Long filenames must truncate intelligently, with a tooltip or details view providing access to the full name.

Do not make metadata so small that it becomes unreadable.

Use font weight to establish hierarchy. Avoid bolding every label.

Numbers used for storage capacity, file counts, and file sizes should align neatly and be formatted consistently.

---

## 6. SPACING, GRID, AND LAYOUT RULES

Create a formal spacing system based on a consistent scale.

Suggested spacing tokens:

- 4 px: micro-spacing.
- 8 px: compact spacing.
- 12 px: standard control spacing.
- 16 px: standard component spacing.
- 20 px: section spacing.
- 24 px: major content spacing.
- 32 px: large layout spacing.
- 40–48 px: major section separation.

Do not apply all spacing values indiscriminately. Choose them according to component relationships.

### 6.1 Desktop layout

The application must be designed primarily for desktop use.

The main window should make effective use of available screen space.

Use a three-part layout:

1. Navigation sidebar.
2. Main workspace.
3. Optional details or preview panel.

The sidebar should remain compact enough to leave sufficient space for files.

The details panel should be collapsible and should not occupy unnecessary space when unused.

### 6.2 Window-size behaviour

Design and test the interface at these representative sizes:

- 1024 × 768.
- 1280 × 720.
- 1366 × 768.
- 1600 × 900.
- 1920 × 1080.

Also test Windows display scaling at 100%, 125%, and 150%.

The application must not depend on one fixed screen resolution.

At smaller widths:

- Reduce unnecessary horizontal spacing.
- Allow the sidebar to collapse.
- Hide optional metadata columns.
- Move secondary controls into overflow menus.
- Preserve access to search and primary navigation.
- Avoid horizontal clipping.

At larger widths:

- Increase the number of visible thumbnails.
- Use available space efficiently.
- Allow optional metadata and preview panels.
- Avoid stretching content excessively.

Do not solve responsive problems by simply shrinking every element.

