# PART V — THE UNIVERSAL PHOTO LIBRARY

## 12. PHOTOS PAGE: THE MOST IMPORTANT SCREEN

The Photos page is the centrepiece of the application.

It must provide an experience comparable in convenience to Google Files on Android, adapted for a large desktop screen.

All discovered images from configured locations should be accessible in a unified library.

### 12.1 Page header

Display:

- Heading: Photos.
- A live image count when available.
- Search or filtering access.
- Sort control.
- Grid/list toggle.
- Selection actions when items are selected.
- Optional indexing status.

The heading should be prominent but compact.

### 12.2 Photo category navigation

Provide accessible filters or a secondary category-navigation area for:

- All Photos.
- Camera.
- Screenshots.
- Downloads.
- Recently Added.
- Favourites.
- Albums.
- Other Images.

Where applicable, show detected source folders as additional filters.

These categories should not appear as empty or fake destinations when no matching files exist. Empty categories may be hidden or shown with a clear empty state, depending on the chosen design.

### 12.3 All Photos

Display images from all configured, accessible, indexed locations.

Use a responsive thumbnail grid with consistent spacing and visually balanced proportions.

The grid should adapt to the available width.

Provide:

- Adjustable thumbnail size.
- A useful default thumbnail size.
- Optional compact mode.
- Optional date grouping.
- Optional filename display.
- Sorting by date, name, size, and other supported metadata.
- Filtering by file type, location, and date.
- Multi-selection.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Context menus.
- Image preview.

Avoid forcing the user into a single rigid grid layout.

### 12.4 Thumbnail treatment

Image thumbnails should be treated as important visual assets.

Requirements:

- Correct aspect-ratio handling.
- Attractive cropping for gallery presentation.
- No distortion.
- Smooth placeholder-to-thumbnail transitions.
- Lazy loading.
- Thumbnail caching.
- Loading indicators when necessary.
- Fallback icons when a preview cannot be generated.
- No repeated placeholder image pretending to represent real content.

Allow users to choose whether thumbnails use a uniform crop or preserve the full image.

For photographic browsing, use subtle corner rounding and minimal surrounding decoration.

Do not put every thumbnail inside a heavy bordered card.

Use a consistent selection indicator that remains visible over bright and dark images.

### 12.5 Camera category

The Camera category should identify images stored in recognised camera-related directories or confidently classified through available metadata.

Possible folder names may include Camera and DCIM/Camera where present.

Do not assume every folder named Pictures contains camera photographs.

Do not claim that an image came from a physical camera unless the available folder information or metadata supports that conclusion.

### 12.6 Screenshots category

Identify screenshots through reliable signals such as:

- Recognised Screenshots directory.
- Relevant file naming patterns.
- Available metadata where practical.

Provide a configurable option for additional screenshot folders.

Do not misclassify every PNG image as a screenshot.

### 12.7 Downloads category

Display images found in indexed Downloads locations.

The category should remain connected to the original Downloads directory.

Do not physically move files into a central folder.

### 12.8 Recently Added

Provide a chronological view of newly discovered or recently added images.

Clearly define which timestamp is being used.

Prefer reliable creation or modification information where appropriate, and do not imply that the date represents when a photograph was originally taken unless EXIF metadata confirms that.

### 12.9 Albums and collections

Allow the user to create custom virtual albums.

For example:

- University.
- Family.
- Wallpapers.
- Projects.
- Receipts.
- Favourite Memories.

Albums should reference original files.

Provide options to add and remove images without moving them.

Where a physical album or folder is explicitly requested, explain whether the action creates a real directory or a virtual collection.

### 12.10 Image viewer

When a photo is opened, provide a dedicated image-viewing experience.

Include:

- Large image display.
- Zoom in and out.
- Fit to window.
- Actual-size viewing.
- Full-screen mode.
- Previous and next image navigation.
- Rotation where supported.
- Filename.
- Dimensions.
- File size.
- Original location.
- Relevant metadata.
- Open-with action.
- Reveal-in-folder action.
- Favourite toggle.
- Context menu.

Image navigation should respect the current collection or search results.

If the user opens an image from Screenshots, Previous and Next should normally move between screenshots rather than jumping to unrelated photos.

### 12.11 Image metadata

Where available, display:

- Width and height.
- File format.
- File size.
- Creation or modification timestamps.
- EXIF information.
- Camera model.
- Date taken.
- Orientation.
- Other useful image metadata.

Do not invent missing metadata.

Provide a clear distinction between filesystem timestamps and metadata embedded in the image.

### 12.12 Supported image formats

Support common formats such as:

- JPEG and JPG.
- PNG.
- WebP.
- GIF.
- BMP.
- TIFF.
- HEIC/HEIF where compatible codecs or libraries are available.
- SVG previews where safely supported.

Animated formats should be handled appropriately.

If a format cannot be previewed, the file should remain discoverable and openable through a compatible external application where available.

